.TH OPENUNLINK 1 "2026-08-01" "openunlink 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
openunlink \(em report stable zero-link regular-file descriptors for one process
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B openunlink
.I PID
.br
.B openunlink \-\-help
.br
.B openunlink \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B openunlink
is a small read-only ISO C17/Linux command-line utility.  It inspects one
explicitly supplied process ID through the fixed
.BR /proc/PID/fd
directory and reports descriptors whose followed targets are observed as stable
regular files with a final
.BR st_nlink
of zero.
.PP
The utility does not open target contents, recover files, estimate reclaimable
storage, terminate processes, or accept an alternate procfs root.  Procfs
visibility and ordinary credential or
.B hidepid
policy apply in the caller's current namespaces.
.PP
The only informational forms are sole-argument
.B \-\-help
and
.BR \-\-version .
The PID must be canonical ASCII decimal text in the range 1 through
.BR INT_MAX .
.SH OUTPUT
Each finding is one stdout line:
.PP
.RS
.nf
OPEN_UNLINKED<TAB>pid=PID<TAB>fd=FD<TAB>size=BYTES<TAB>target="ESCAPED_TARGET"
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B BYTES
is the lossless final
.B st_size
observation.  It is not the procfs link-text length, a reclaim estimate, or a
physical allocation measurement.  Procfs-supplied display bytes are escaped;
the literal
.B \ (deleted)
suffix is never used as the finding predicate.
.PP
The scanner retains the first 65536 valid descriptor names in observed
enumeration order and sorts those retained names numerically before inspection.
The 65537th valid name stops enumeration and produces one
.BR FD_COUNT_LIMIT
advisory without discarding findings already retained.
.SH LINK-COUNT BOUNDARY
The finding predicate is the final followed metadata observation
.BR st_nlink == 0
after target identity and regular-file type remain stable around link-text
inspection.  A status-0 result therefore means only that no retained,
successfully inspected descriptor met that narrow observation.
.PP
NFS silly-rename and other filesystem or stacking behavior can leave a nonzero
link count after an unlink-like operation.  Those cases are intentionally
silent.  A cosmetic
.B \ (deleted)
suffix in procfs text is likewise only display context.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
No retained descriptor produced a finding, or an informational command
succeeded.
.TP
.B 1
At least one
.B OPEN_UNLINKED
finding was emitted; per-descriptor advisories may also be present.
.TP
.B 2
Usage, process, scan, memory, size, or checked output failure.
.SH NON-GOALS
The utility does not follow caller-provided paths, open target files, group
descriptors by inode, modify processes or filesystems, read configuration,
persist state, run a service, use networking, or claim file recovery.  It is a
source preview and is not installed by
.BR make\ install .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR proc (5)
.SH AUTHORITATIVE CONTRACT
See
.BR docs/sixth-utility-capability-contract.md
in the source tree.
